# redis-search on Olares

The Olares One box (`ssh olares`, 192.168.4.153, Olares OS 1.12.6, K3s + containerd) runs a
self-contained redis-search stack as an Olares app named `redissearch`
(namespace `redissearch-2019065722`, Olares ID `2019065722@olares.com`).

## Why not the built-in Olares Redis

Olares' Redis middleware is Apache Kvrocks (`os-platform/kvrocks-0`). It cannot load Redis
modules and its native FT.* subset lacks `ON JSON` indexes and `FT.ALTER`, both of which this
repo uses. The app runs its own `redis/redis-stack-server:7.4.0-v8`, pinned to the GCP VM
version.

## Layout

- Chart: `deploy/olares/redissearch/` (Helm chart + `OlaresManifest.yaml`, manifest 0.12.0 / `apiVersion: v3`).
- Workloads: `redis` (Redis Stack), `redissearch` (web, `docker/cloudrun.Dockerfile`, plus the
  public Redis IAP tunnel sidecar),
  `etl` (`docker/etl.Dockerfile` in `cron` mode: MM warmup 2 AM, ETL 3 AM Eastern).
- Ops: `scripts/olares.sh`, wrapped by `make olares-*`.
- Docker is not used on the box. Web/ETL images are built locally for `linux/amd64` and imported
  into the system containerd (`/run/containerd`, `k8s.io` namespace; Olares K3s does not use
  the K3s-embedded containerd, so `k3s ctr` fails) with `imagePullPolicy: Never`. Pods always run
  the mutable `:olares` tag; every build is also tagged `:<git sha>[-dirty-<hash>]` for rollback.

## Olares chart rules enforced at upload (1.12.6)

- Every Deployment's `spec.replicas` must be `{{ .Values.workloads.<name>.replicaCount }}`, with the
  value defined in `values.yaml` and matching `workloadReplicas` in the manifest.
- Deployments that mount `hostPath` volumes must use `strategy: Recreate`.
- The sum of all containers' requests/limits must fit `spec.accelerator` (`requiredCpu`,
  `limitedCpu`, `requiredMemory`, `limitedMemory`) in the manifest; raise those when adding containers.
- Upgrades reuse the installed release's values, so keys added to `values.yaml` later render as nil
  and fail the upgrade. Put new settings in templates as literals (or reinstall).
- After a failed upgrade, re-uploading the same chart version does not start a new operation (the
  watcher echoes the old failure). Bump the chart version and upgrade again.

## Storage and secrets

Everything lives in the app Data folder (`.Values.userspace.appData`,
`/olares/rootfs/userspace/pvc-userspace-2019065722-*/Data/redissearch`), which survives uninstall:

| Path | Contents |
|---|---|
| `config/` (root, 0700) | `env` (rendered from `config/local.env` + Olares overrides), `gcp-key.json`, `redis_password` |
| `redis/` | `dump.rdb`, `appendonlydir/` |
| `etl-logs/` | Dated ETL logs and daily start stamps (late-start catch-up relies on them) |

`config/` is mounted read-only at `/secrets` in every pod. The web image already sources
`/secrets/env`; the ETL container sources it before `entrypoint.sh cron`. After editing
`config/local.env`, run `make olares-config` then restart the pods (`make olares-import` or a
rollout restart). The Redis password is generated once and reused on later `olares-config` runs.

Olares overrides written into `env`: `REDIS_HOST=redis`, `REDIS_PORT=6379`, generated
`REDIS_PASSWORD`, `GCS_ETL_PREFIX=redis-search/etl/olares`,
`GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/secrets/gcp-key.json`, `ENVIRONMENT=local`.

`MEDIA_MANAGER_API_URL` is set by the chart, not the env file: the web and etl containers point at
the box's Media Manager app (`mediamanager`, deployed from the media-manager repo) at
`http://$(HOST_IP):48098`, where `HOST_IP` is the node IP from the Downward API (`status.hostIP`).
See "Calling between Olares apps" below.

## Networking

- Web: Olares entrance `redissearch` (appid `08dca9d2`), `authLevel: internal`.
  - LAN, no login: `http://08dca9d2.2019065722.olares.local`
  - `https://08dca9d2.2019065722.olares.com` resolves to the public Olares relay, so it is treated
    as external and redirects to Olares login unless DNS points it at 192.168.4.153 (or via LarePass VPN).
  - `.local` costs 2-5s of mDNS lookup per new connection on macOS; avoid it for API clients.
- `make olares-tunnel` serves the web API on `localhost:9001` (SSH + `kubectl port-forward`, box port
  = local port + 10000) and the box Redis on `localhost:6380` (SSH to the `46379` listener).
  Hitting the Service IP from the box host returns 401 (Olares auth proxy), hence port-forward.
- Redis: TCP `exposePort: 46379` on 192.168.4.153 (LAN/VPN only), password in
  `config/redis_password` on the box.
- Web API for other apps: TCP `exposePort: 47438` (manifest port `web-api`, container `api-forward`).
- Outbound: Olares allows HTTP(S) egress; SMTP 587 (SendGrid) is opened via
  `options.allowedOutboundPorts`. Any new non-HTTP egress port must be added there.

## Calling between Olares apps

Pods in another app (e.g. `mediamanager`) cannot call this app's Services directly:

- Each app namespace gets an `app-np` NetworkPolicy, owned by app-service's security controller, that
  only admits system namespaces, the user's internal namespace, the app's own namespace and the host
  proxy (`10.233.98.0/32`). NetworkPolicies shipped in a chart are deleted by that controller.
- Entrance pods get an envoy sidecar that redirects all inbound traffic to Authelia; pod-to-pod HTTP
  gets `400 cannot get user name from header` (or 401). Loopback traffic is not intercepted, and
  ports declared in the manifest `ports:` are exempt.

Pattern used by both apps: a forwarder container listens on a declared port and relays to
`127.0.0.1:<app port>`; the declared port has an `exposePort`, and callers use
`<node IP>:<exposePort>` (node IP via `status.hostIP`). redis-search: 18080 -> 8080, exposed on
47438. Media Manager: 18098 -> 8080, exposed on 48098. These ports skip Olares auth, so they are
LAN-open like Redis on 46379; the 8080 entrances keep their login check for external access.

Olares fixes `exposePort` the first time a port is assigned and ignores later edits, so the manifest
pins the assigned value. `appScope.clusterScoped`/`appRef` sharing needs a cluster-scoped reinstall
and was not used.

## mediacircle local development against the box

mediacircle's emulator code cannot point at a remote host: the search clients hard-code
`http://localhost:9001` (ignoring `REDIS_SEARCH_API_URL`), and the Redis cache clients
(`utils/redis_cache.py`, `utils/redis-cache.ts`) force the host to `localhost` and only send
`REDIS_PASSWORD` to localhost. So the box must appear on the Mac's localhost via `olares-tunnel`,
replacing the local Docker stack on the same ports. The only mediacircle change is the password.

| Step | To the box | Back to local Docker |
|---|---|---|
| redis-search | `make docker-down-all`, then `make olares-tunnel` (leave running) | Ctrl-C the tunnel, `make web-docker` |
| mediacircle `config/local.env` | add `REDIS_PASSWORD=<make olares-redis-password>` | remove `REDIS_PASSWORD` (local Redis has none; AUTH would fail) |
| mediacircle | restart dev (`dev-start.sh` copies `local.env` into the functions `.env` files) | restart dev |

`REDIS_HOST=localhost`, `REDIS_PORT=6380`, `REDIS_SEARCH_API_URL=http://localhost:9001` stay as they
are. mediacircle Makefile targets that `unset REDIS_PASSWORD` (`inspect-generic-pools-local`,
`taste-profile-redis-resync-local`) fail auth against the box.

## Logs

`make olares-logs [SVC=web|iap|redis|etl] [FOLLOW=0] [TAIL=n]` tails `kubectl logs` over SSH
(default: web, follow, last 200 lines). The ETL cron tees its dated files in `etl-logs/` to stdout,
so `SVC=etl` covers nightly runs; the files persist across pod restarts, the stream does not.

## Public Redis

- Public Redis (`redis-stack-vm`, internal IP only): the web pod's `public-redis-tunnel` container
  runs `gcloud compute start-iap-tunnel` on `localhost:6381`, so the code's `PUBLIC_REDIS_HOST/PORT`
  defaults work unchanged and the web UI's environment switcher reaches public Redis.
  - Image `redissearch-iap:olares` (`docker/iap-tunnel.Dockerfile`, gcloud only) is built and
    imported separately with `make olares-iap-image`, so `olares-deploy` does not ship the Cloud SDK.
    `olares-install`/`olares-upgrade` refuse to run if it is missing from the box.
  - Auth: `/secrets/gcp-key.json` (media-manager SA) via `CLOUDSDK_AUTH_CREDENTIAL_FILE_OVERRIDE`.
    The SA holds `roles/iap.tunnelResourceAccessor` on the project with condition
    `destination.port == 6379`, and `roles/compute.viewer` on the instance (gcloud looks up the VM).
  - The container has no probes and loops on exit, so an IAP outage never marks the web pod unready.
    Tunnel logs: `kubectl logs -n redissearch-2019065722 deploy/redissearch -c public-redis-tunnel`.
  - `/api/switch-redis` changes the active Redis for every client of the box's API (including
    mediacircle via `olares-tunnel`), not just the browser session.

## Seeding Redis from a snapshot

Redis 7.4 ignores `dump.rdb` when it starts with `appendonly yes` and no AOF exists (it creates an
empty AOF and later overwrites the snapshot). The `redis` container therefore starts with
`appendonly no` when `/data/appendonlydir` is missing and `/data/dump.rdb` exists, then issues
`CONFIG SET appendonly yes` once the dataset is loaded. Do not remove that logic.

`make olares-seed` BGSAVEs the local Docker Redis, streams `dump.rdb` to the box, and verifies the
sha256. It refuses to run while Redis is running on the box, and refuses to replace existing data
without `FORCE=1` (the old `appendonlydir` is moved aside, not deleted).

## ETL resume state

The box uses its own GCS prefix `redis-search/etl/olares` so it never advances the Mac (`local`)
or dev resume dates. With no state, the ETL starts from yesterday, so after seeding run one
catch-up from the day before the snapshot's newest document (printed by `olares-seed`):

```bash
make olares-etl-catchup START=YYYY-MM-DD
```

That run records the first `olares` state; the nightly cron resumes from it.

## First deploy

```bash
make olares-config
make olares-seed            # before install: Redis is not running yet
make olares-build
make olares-import
make olares-iap-image
# one-time, on the box: olares-cli profile login --olares-id 2019065722@olares.com
make olares-install
make olares-etl-catchup START=YYYY-MM-DD
make olares-status
```

## Deploying code changes

There is no live reload on the box (unlike the local `web` container's mounted source +
`uvicorn --reload`); code ships inside the images.

```bash
make olares-deploy                 # build working tree, import, restart web + ETL, check /health
make olares-rollback               # list deploy history and tags on the box
make olares-rollback TAG=<tag>     # retag :olares to <tag> on the box and restart
```

- The tag is the git short sha; uncommitted changes add `-dirty-<hash of the diff>`, so a dirty deploy
  is visibly not reproducible from a commit.
- Deploy/rollback refuse to run 2-5 AM Eastern or while `etl.run_nightly_etl` is running in the etl pod,
  because restarting the etl pod kills the run. `ALLOW_ETL_WINDOW=1` overrides.
- History is appended to `deploy-history` in the app Data folder.
- Old sha tags accumulate in containerd; they share base layers, so only changed layers cost space.

| Change | Command |
|---|---|
| Python code, `requirements.txt` | `make olares-deploy` |
| `config/local.env` values | `make olares-config`, then `make olares-deploy` (or restart pods) |
| Chart (resources, ports, Redis flags) | `make olares-upgrade` |

Chart changes: bump `version` in `Chart.yaml` and `metadata.version`/`spec.versionName` in
`OlaresManifest.yaml` (same-version re-upload is also accepted for the `upload` source), then
`make olares-upgrade`.

## Stopping and starting

`make olares-stop` / `make olares-start` wrap `olares-cli market stop` / `resume` (needs the
`olares-cli profile login` session on the box). Data in the app Data folder is untouched.

- Stop is guarded like deploy (ETL window / active run) and then waits for pods to exit: Olares reports
  `stopped` before pods terminate, and `olares-seed` must not overwrite files under a live Redis.
- The backend returns HTTP 500 for stop-when-stopped, so both targets check state first and are no-ops
  in the requested state.
- Start waits for Redis readiness (the AOF load of ~1.5M keys takes ~1.5 min) and web `/health`.
- A stopped app misses the 3 AM ETL; after a long stop run `make olares-etl-catchup START=<date>`.
